== Chapter XIII

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[Several ways in which the bringing forth of conceived sin is obstructed.]
]

#smallcaps[Before] we go on to the remaining evidences of the power and efficacy of the #emph[law of sin,] we will take occasion from what has been said to turn aside to one consideration. It arises from the Scripture that was made the basis and foundation of our discourse on the general #emph[deceitfulness of sin,] namely Jas 1:14. The apostle tells us that “lust, when it has conceived, gives birth to sin.” He seems to imply that whatever sin is conceived is also brought forth. Now we have placed the conception of sin in the #emph[consent of the will] to it, and we rightly reckon that the bringing forth of sin consists in its #emph[actual commission.] On that account we know that the one does not necessarily follow the other. There is a world of sin conceived in the womb of men’s wills and hearts that is never brought forth. Our present business, then, will be to inquire how that comes about. I answer, then:

First, that conceived sin is not always brought forth is no thanks to sin, nor to its law. What it conceives, it would bring forth. That it does not is, for the most part, only a small lessening of its guilt. A #emph[settled will] to sin actually is actual sin. Nothing is lacking on sin’s part to make every conceived sin actually accomplished. The obstacle and hindrance lie elsewhere.

Secondly, two things are necessary in the creature that has #emph[conceived sin] if it is to bring it forth. First, #emph[power.] Secondly, #emph[continuance in the will to sin] until the sin is carried out and committed. Where these #emph[two] are present, actual sin will unavoidably follow. It is evident, therefore, that whatever hinders conceived sin from being brought forth must affect either the #emph[power] or the #emph[will] of the sinner. This must be from God. And he has two ways of doing it. First, by his #emph[providence,] by which he obstructs the #emph[power] to sin. Secondly, by his #emph[grace,] by which he turns aside or changes the #emph[will] to sin. I do not name these ways of God’s dealings so separately as though the one were always without the other. There is much #emph[grace] in his providential dealings, and much of the #emph[wisdom] of providence is seen in the dispensations of grace. But I place them in this distinction because each is most eminent in its own field: providence in outward acts that concern the power of the creature; grace, common or special, in an inward efficacy that concerns his will. We will begin with the first.

=== God’s Providence Opposed to the Sinner’s Power

First, when sin is conceived, the Lord obstructs its production by his providence. He does it by taking away, or cutting short, the #emph[power] that is absolutely necessary for bringing it forth or accomplishing it. For example:

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, #emph[life] is the foundation of all power, the principle of all action. When life ceases, all power ceases with it. God himself, to show the everlasting stability of his own power, takes the title of the #emph[living God.] Now he often forestalls the power of carrying out sin actually by cutting short and taking away the #emph[lives] of those who have conceived it. So he dealt with the army of #emph[Sennacherib.] Sennacherib had purposed, and accordingly threatened, that “the Lord could not deliver Jerusalem from his hand” (2 Kgs 18:35). God threatens to cut short his #emph[power,] so that he should not carry out his intention (ch. 19:28). He does this accordingly by taking away the #emph[lives of his soldiers] (v. 35), without whom it was impossible for Sennacherib’s conceived sin to be brought forth. #emph[Moses] sets out this providential dealing in obstructing conceived sin excellently in the case of #emph[Pharaoh] (Exod 15:9, 10): “The enemy declared, ‘I will pursue, I will overtake. I will divide the spoils; I will gorge myself on them. I will draw my sword; my hand will destroy them.’ But You blew with Your breath, and the sea covered them. They sank like lead in the mighty waters.” Sin’s conception is fully expressed, and as full a prevention is joined to it. In the same way he dealt with the companies of #emph[fifty] and their #emph[captains] who came to seize #emph[Elijah] (2 Kgs 1:10, 11). #emph[Fire] came down from heaven and consumed them when they were ready to take him. Many other instances of the same kind could be recorded. We have one that concerns all mankind in the great #emph[providential change] that set a limit to the lives of men. Men who lived #emph[hundreds of years] had a long time to bring forth the sins they had conceived. As a result the earth was filled with violence, injustice, and plunder, and all “flesh had corrupted their ways” (Gen 6:12, 19). To prevent a similar flood of sin, God shortens the course of men’s pilgrimage on the earth and reduces their lives to a much shorter measure. Besides this general law, God daily cuts off in this way persons who had conceived much mischief and violence in their hearts, and prevents them from carrying it out. “Men of bloodshed and deceit will not live out half their days.” They still have much work to do, if only they were given time to carry out the bloody and sinful purposes of their minds. The #emph[psalmist] tells us (Ps 146:4), “When his spirit departs, he returns to the ground; on that very day his plans perish.” The man had many schemes about sin, but now they are all cut off. So also Eccl 8:12, 13: “Although a sinner does evil a hundred times and still lives long, yet I also know that it will go well with those who fear God, who are reverent in His presence. Yet because the wicked do not fear God, it will not go well with them, and their days will not lengthen like a shadow.” However long a wicked man lives, he dies #emph[by God’s judgment,] and will not remain to do the evil he had conceived.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[But since we have granted that even #emph[believers] themselves may conceive sin through its power and deceitfulness, it may be asked whether God ever #emph[forestalls] its production and accomplishment in them in this way. Does he cut off and take away their lives, so that they will not be able to carry it out? I answer:]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, God does not #emph[in judgment] cut off and take away the life of any of #emph[his own] for this end and purpose: to prevent the carrying out, or bringing forth, of some particular sin that the person had conceived, and that he would have committed if his life had not been #emph[taken away.] For:]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, this is directly contrary to the declared end of the #emph[patience of God] toward them (2 Pet 3:9). This is the very end of the #emph[long-suffering of God toward believers]: that before they depart from here they may come to the #emph[sense, acknowledgment,] and repentance of every known sin. This is the constant and unchangeable rule of God’s patience in the covenant of grace. It is so far from being an encouragement to sin in them that it is a motive to watchfulness against every sin, of the same nature as all gospel grace and as mercy in the blood of Christ. Now the dealing we are speaking of would directly contradict it.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, this also follows from the first. #emph[Conceived sin] contains the whole nature of sin, as our Savior declares at length (Matt 5). And to be cut off under its guilt, in order to prevent its further progress, implies continuing in the purpose of it without repentance. So those who are cut off in this way #emph[in judgment] cannot but perish forever. But God does not deal so with his own; he does not cast off the people whom he foreknew. That is why #emph[David] prays for the patience of God just mentioned, that it might not be so with him (Ps 39:13): “Spare me, that I may recover strength before I depart and am no more.” But yet:]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, there are some cases in which God may, and does, take away the #emph[lives of his own] to prevent the guilt they would otherwise be involved in. For example:]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, when some great temptation and trial is coming upon the world. God knows that this or that one of his own would not be able to withstand it and hold out against it, but would dishonor him and defile himself. So he may, and no doubt often does, take them out of the world, to take them out of its way (Isa 57:1): “the righteous are taken away from the evil to come.” This means not only the evil of punishment and judgment, but the evil of temptations and trials, which often proves much the worse of the two. In the same way a captain in war will call a soldier off from his watch and guard when he knows that, through some weakness, the soldier is not able to bear the pressure and force of the enemy coming upon him.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, when they have entered on some way not acceptable to him, through ignorance, or through not knowing his mind and will. This seems to have been the case with #emph[Josiah.] And no doubt the Lord often deals with his own in this way. When any of #emph[his own] are engaged in ways that do not please him, through the darkness and ignorance of their minds, he calls them off from their station and employment, so that they may not go on to further evil or mischief. He takes them to himself, where they will err and mistake no more. But in ordinary cases God has other ways of turning his own from sin than by killing them, as we will see afterward.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, God in his providence hinders the bringing forth of conceived sin by taking away, and #emph[cutting short, the power of those who had conceived it.] Their lives continue, but they will not have the #emph[power] without which it is impossible for them to carry out what they had intended, or to bring forth what they had conceived. We have several instances of this as well. Such was the case with the #emph[builders of Babel] (Gen 11). Whatever it was in particular that they aimed at, it was in pursuit of a design of apostasy from God. One thing needed to accomplish their aim was the #emph[oneness of their language.] So God says (#emph[v.] 6), “If they have begun to do this as one people speaking the same language, then nothing they devise will be beyond them.” In the ordinary course of things they will accomplish their wicked design. What course does God now take to forestall their conceived sin? Does he bring a flood on them to destroy them, as on the old world some time before? Does he send his angel to cut them off, as with the army of #emph[Sennacherib] afterward? Does he take away their lives by any means? No. Their lives are continued, but he #emph[confuses their language,] so that they cannot go on with their work (v. 7). He takes away the thing in which their power lay. In the same way he dealt with the #emph[men of Sodom] (Gen 19:11). They were engaged in and set on the pursuit of their filthy lusts. God strikes them with #emph[blindness,] so that they could not find the door where they intended to use violence to get what they wanted. Their #emph[lives] were continued, and their will to sin, but their #emph[power] is cut short and curtailed. His dealing with #emph[Jeroboam] was of the same nature (1 Kgs 13:4). He “stretched out his hand” to seize the prophet, and it withered and became useless. And this is a notable way in which God’s providence acts effectually in the world to stop that flood of sin which would overflow all the earth if every womb of it were opened. He cuts men short of the #emph[moral] power by which they would accomplish it. Many a wretch who has conceived mischief against the church of God has by this means been stripped of the #emph[power] by which he thought to accomplish it. Some have their bodies struck with diseases, so that they can no longer serve their lusts or go along with them in committing folly. Some are deprived of the instruments with which they would work. For a long time there has been sin and mischief enough conceived to root out the generation of the righteous from the face of the earth, if men had strength and ability to match their will, and if God did not cut off and shorten their power and the days of their prevailing. Ps 64:6: “They devise injustice and say, ‘We have perfected a secret plan.’ For the inner man and the heart are mysterious.” Everything is in readiness; the design is well laid; their counsels are deep and secret. What will now hinder them from doing whatever they have imagined? #emph[Vv.] 7, 8: “But God will shoot them with arrows; suddenly they will be wounded. They will be made to stumble, their own tongues turned against them.” God meets them and brings them down, so that they will not be able to accomplish their design. This way of God’s preventing sin seems to belong, at least ordinarily, to the men of the world. God deals with them so every day, and leaves them to waste away in their sins. They go all their days big with the iniquity they have conceived, and are greatly burdened that they cannot be delivered of it. The prophet tells us that #emph[they accomplish] the iniquity they had conceived “because the power is in their hands” (Mic 2:1). If they have power for it, they will accomplish it. Ezek 22:6: “has used his power to shed blood.” This is the measure of their sinning: their power. Many of them do no more evil, commit no more sin, only because they cannot. Their whole restraint lies in being cut short in power in one way or another. Their bodies will not serve them for the uncleanness they have planned, nor their hands for their revenge and plunder, nor their instruments for persecution. But they go burdened with conceived sin, and are disquieted and tortured by it all their days. That is why they become, to themselves as well as to others, “like the storm-tossed sea, for it cannot be still” (Isa 57:20).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[It may also be that in some cases, under some violent temptations or in mistakes, God forestalls the accomplishment of conceived sin in his own in this way. There seems to be an instance of it in his dealing with #emph[Jehoshaphat.] Against the mind of God he had planned to join in alliance with #emph[Ahab,] and to send his ships with him to #emph[Tarshish.] But God breaks his ships by a wind, so that he could not accomplish what he had planned. But when God deals with his own in this way, there is a difference from the same dealing with others. For:]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, it is so only in cases of #emph[extraordinary temptation.] When through the violence of temptation and the craft of Satan they are hurried out from under the rule of the law of grace, God in one way or another takes away their power, or may do so, so that they will not be able to carry out what they had planned. But with wicked men this is an ordinary way of dealing. This hook of God is in them through the whole course of their lives. They struggle with it, like a wild bull in a net (Isa 51:20). God’s #emph[net] is upon them, and they are filled with fury that they cannot do all the wickedness they would.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, God does not do it to leave his own wrestling with sin and trying other ways of accomplishing it when the way they were engaged in has failed. Rather, by their disappointment he awakens them to consider their condition and what they are doing. And so he #emph[consumes sin in the womb] by the ways that will be dwelt on afterward. For some men, being deprived of power to commit a sin they had conceived and contrived has been sanctified to change their hearts from all toying with that sin or any other.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, God #emph[in his providence] hinders the bringing forth of conceived sin by setting an #emph[outward hindering power] against sinners. He leaves them their lives, and leaves them power to do what they intend, but he raises up an opposing power to coerce, forbid, and restrain them. We have an instance of this in 1 Sam 14:35. #emph[Saul] had sworn that #emph[Jonathan] should be put to death, and as far as appears he went on resolutely to kill him. God stirs up the spirit of the people; they set themselves against the wrath and fury of #emph[Saul,] and #emph[Jonathan] is delivered. So also 2 Chr 26:7, 8, 19. When King #emph[Uzziah] would have offered incense in his own person contrary to the law, #emph[eighty of the priests] resisted him and drove him out of the temple. Under this head belong all the help God stirs up to deliver his people from the fury of persecutors. He raises up “deliverers on Mount Zion to rule over the mountains of Esau.” So in Rev 12:16: the dragon, and those acting under him and moved by his spirit, were striving furiously to destroy the church. God stirs up the #emph[earth] to help her, that is, men of the world who are not joined with the others in Satan’s design. By their opposition he hinders the others from carrying out the rage they had planned. God’s dealing with his own people in Hos 2:6, 7 seems to be of this nature. They were pursuing their iniquities, going after their lovers. God leaves them for a while to act in the folly of their spirits, but he sets a #emph[hedge] and a #emph[wall] before them, so that they will not be able to fulfill their designs and lusts.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Fourthly, God forestalls the accomplishment of conceived sin by removing, or taking away, the #emph[objects] on whom, or concerning whom, the conceived sin was to be committed. Acts 12:11 gives us a notable instance of this outcome of providence. When the day was coming on which #emph[Herod] intended to kill #emph[Peter,] who was shut up in prison, God sends and takes Peter away from their rage and their lying in wait. So too our #emph[Savior] himself was taken away from the murderous rage of the #emph[Jews] before his hour had come (John 8:59; ch. 10:39). Both #emph[early] and #emph[later] times are full of stories of this kind. Prison doors have been opened, and poor creatures appointed to die have often been rescued from the jaws of death. In the world itself, among its own men, adulterers and adulteresses, the sin of the one is often hindered and stifled by the taking away of the other. So #emph[wings] were given to the #emph[woman] to carry her into the wilderness, and to frustrate the world in carrying out its rage (Rev 12:14).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Fifthly, God does this by some notable #emph[diversions] of the thoughts of men who had conceived sin (Gen 37:24). The #emph[brothers of Joseph] threw him into a pit, intending to starve him there. While they were, it seems, pleasing themselves with what they had done, God arranges for a company of merchants to come by. With that new #emph[object] he turns their thoughts from #emph[killing] their brother to #emph[selling] him (vv. 26, 27). And we know how far in this they served the infinitely wise counsel of God. So also when #emph[Saul] was pursuing #emph[David,] and was just about to prevail against him to his destruction, God stirs up the #emph[Philistines] to invade the land. This both turned Saul’s thoughts aside and drew the course of his actions another way (1 Sam 23:27).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[These are #emph[some of the ways] in which God is pleased to hinder the bringing forth of #emph[conceived sin] by setting himself and his providence against the #emph[power of the sinning creature.] And in passing we may take a brief look at the great advantages to faith and to the church of God that may be found in this matter. For example:]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, this may give us a little insight into the providence of God, ever to be adored, which by these and similar ways, in great variety, obstructs the breaking out of sin in the world. It is he who builds those dams and shuts those floodgates of corrupted nature, so that it does not break out in a deluge of filthy abominations to overwhelm the creation with confusion and disorder. As it was of old, so it is today: “every inclination of the thoughts of man’s heart is altogether evil all the time.” That all the earth is not filled in every place with violence, as it was of old, is solely from the mighty hand of God working effectually to obstruct sin. It is from this alone that the highways, streets, and fields are not all filled with violence, blood, plunder, uncleanness, and every villainy the heart of man can conceive. O the infinite beauty of divine wisdom and providence in the government of the world! For preserving it daily requires no less power and wisdom than its first making did.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, if we look to our own concerns, they will in a special way press us to adore the wisdom and efficacy of God’s providence in stopping the progress of #emph[conceived sin.] That we are at peace in our houses, at rest in our beds, and have any quiet in what we enjoy, comes from this alone. Whose person would not be defiled or destroyed? Whose home would not be ruined? Whose blood, almost, would not be shed, if wicked men had power to carry out all the sin they conceive? It may be that the ruin of some of us has been conceived a thousand times. We are indebted to this providence that obstructs sin for our lives, our families, our estates, our liberties, and whatever is or may be dear to us. For may we not sometimes say with the psalmist (Ps 57:4), “My soul is among the lions; I lie among those who are set on fire—with men whose teeth are spears and arrows, whose tongues are sharp swords.” And how is deliverance from such persons brought about? Ps 58:6: “God shatters their teeth in their mouths, the fangs of the young lions.” He keeps this fire from burning, or quenches it when it is ready to break out into a flame. He breaks their spears and arrows, so that sometimes we are not even wounded by them. Some he cuts off and destroys; some he cuts short in their power; some he deprives of the instruments by which alone they can work; some he keeps from the opportunities they desire, or turns aside by other objects for their lusts; and often he makes them spend their lusts among themselves, one upon another. We may say, therefore, with the #emph[psalmist] (Ps 104:24), “How many are Your works, O #smallcaps[Lord]! In wisdom You have made them all; the earth is full of Your creatures”\; and with the prophet (Hos 14:9), “Whoever is wise, let him understand these things; whoever is discerning, let him know them. For the ways of the #smallcaps[Lord] are right, and the righteous walk in them, but the rebellious stumble in them.”]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, if these and similar ways are the ways in which God forestalls the bringing forth of conceived sin in wicked men, we may learn from this how miserable their condition is, and in what constant #emph[torment] they spend their days, for the most part. They are “like the storm-tossed sea,” says the Lord, “for it cannot be still.” As they try to make sure that others have no peace, so it is certain that they themselves have none. The principle of sin is not impaired or weakened in them; the will to sin is not taken away. They have a #emph[womb of sin] that is able to #emph[conceive] monsters every moment. Indeed, for the most part they are forging and framing folly all day long. They are contriving how to satisfy one lust or another. They are devouring by malice and revenge, or corrupting by uncleanness, or trampling by ambition, or swallowing up by covetousness, all who stand before them. Many of their follies and mischiefs they bring to the very point of birth, and are in pain to be delivered. But God fills them with disappointment every day, and #emph[shuts up the womb] of sin. Some are filled with hatred of God’s people all their days, and never once have an opportunity to act on it. So #emph[David] describes them (Ps 59:6): “They return in the evening, snarling like dogs and prowling around the city.” Verse 7: “See what they spew from their mouths—sharp words from their lips.” And yet they are not able to accomplish their designs. What torture do such poor creatures live in! Envy, malice, wrath, and revenge devour their hearts for want of an outlet. And when God has used the other acts of his wise providence, cutting short their power or setting a greater power against them, and nothing else will do, he cuts them off in their sins, and to the grave they go full of purposes of iniquity. Others are no less driven and pulled about by the power of other lusts that they are not able to satisfy. This is the heavy labor they are occupied with all their days. If they accomplish their designs, they are more wicked and hellish than before; and if they do not, they are filled with vexation and discontent. This is the portion of those who do not know the Lord, nor the power of his grace. Do not envy their condition. Whatever their outward glittering show, their hearts are full of anxiety, trouble, and sorrow.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Fourthly, do we sometimes see the floodgates of men’s lusts and rage opened against the church and its cause? Does success go with them, and is power on their side for a season? Let not the saints of God lose heart. He has unspeakably varied and effectual ways of #emph[stifling what they have conceived,] to give them dry breasts and a miscarrying womb. He can stop their fury when he pleases. “Surely,” says the psalmist, “the wrath of man shall praise You; the remainder of wrath You will restrain” (Ps 76:10). When so much of their wrath has been let out as will exalt his praise, he can, when he pleases, set up a power greater than the combined strength of all sinning creatures, and restrain the remainder of the wrath they had conceived. “He breaks the spirits of princes; He is feared by the kings of the earth” (v. 12). Some he will cut off and destroy, some he will terrify and frighten, and he will prevent the rage of them all. He can knock them on the head, or break out their teeth, or chain up their wrath; and who can oppose him?]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Fifthly, those who have received benefit by any of the ways mentioned may know to whom they owe their preservation, and not look on it as a common thing. When you have #emph[conceived sin,] has God weakened your power for sin, or denied you opportunity, or taken away the object of your lusts, or turned your thoughts aside by new providences? Know assuredly that you have received mercy by it. God does not always deal out these providences in subservience to the covenant of grace, yet there is always #emph[mercy] in them, and always a call in them to consider their Author. If God had not dealt with you in this way, perhaps today you would have been a terror to yourself, a shame to your relatives, and under the punishment due to some notorious sins you had #emph[conceived.] Besides, there is commonly an #emph[added guilt] in sin brought forth, beyond what there is in its mere conception. Perhaps others would have been ruined by it here, or drawn into partnership in the sin by it, and so been ruined eternally by it. All this is prevented by these providences, and eternity will bear witness that there is a singular mercy in them. Do not look on such things, then, as common accidents. The hand of God is in them all, and it is a merciful hand, if it is not despised. If it is despised, God still does good to others by it: the world is the better for it, and you are not as wicked as you would be.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Sixthly, we may also see from this the great use of #emph[magistracy] in the world, that great appointment of God. Among other things it serves this holy providence in a special way, in obstructing the bringing forth of #emph[conceived sin]\; it does so by the terror of him who bears the sword. God fixes on the hearts of evil men what he expresses in Rom 13:4: “But if you do wrong, be afraid, for he does not carry the sword in vain. He is God’s servant, an agent of retribution to the wrongdoer.” God fixes this on the hearts of men, and by its dread and terror #emph[closes the womb of sin,] so that it does not bring forth. When there was “no king in Israel,” no one to rebuke, and no one whom evil men feared, woeful work and havoc was made among the children of men in the world, as we may see in the last chapters of the book of #emph[Judges.] The greatest mercies and blessings we partake of in this world, next to those of the gospel and the covenant of grace, come to us through this channel and conduit. Indeed, what we have been speaking of is the proper work of #emph[magistracy]: to serve the providence of God in obstructing the bringing forth of conceived sin.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[These, then, are some of the ways in which God #emph[in his providence] prevents the #emph[bringing forth of sin] by setting obstacles against the #emph[power] of the sinner. By them sin is not consumed, but shut up in the womb. Men are not burdened #emph[for] it, but #emph[with] it. They are not weighed down in heart and conscience with its #emph[guilt,] but perplexed with its #emph[power,] which they are not able to exercise and satisfy.]]

=== God’s Working on the Will of the Sinner

The way that still remains to be considered, by which God forestalls the production of #emph[conceived sin,] is his working on the #emph[will] of sinners, so making sin waste away in the womb.

In general there are two ways in which God prevents the bringing forth of conceived sin by working #emph[on the will of the sinner.] They are, first, by #emph[restraining grace]\; secondly, by #emph[renewing grace.] He does it sometimes in the one way, sometimes in the other. The first is common to regenerate and unregenerate persons; the second belongs only to believers. And by both of them God works in varied ways as to particulars. We will begin with the first.

First, God does this in the way of restraining grace by some #emph[arrow of particular conviction] fixed in the heart and conscience of the sinner, concerning the particular sin he had conceived. This staggers and changes his mind about the particular thing intended. It makes his hands hang down, and the weapons of lust fall out of them. By this #emph[conceived sin] proves abortive. How God does this work, by what immediate touches, strokes, blows, and rebukes of his Spirit, by what reasonings, arguments, and stirrings of men’s own consciences, is not for us to find out fully. It is done, as was said, in unspeakable variety, and the works of God are past finding out. But after we have shown the general way of God’s proceeding, we will dwell on what light may be given to it from instances in Scripture.

This, then, was how God dealt in the case of #emph[Esau] and #emph[Jacob. Esau] had long conceived his brother’s death. He comforted himself with the thought of it and with resolutions about it (Gen 27:41), as is the manner of profligate sinners. At his first opportunity he comes out to carry out the rage he intended, and #emph[Jacob] concludes that he would “attack the mothers and children” (Gen 32:11). An opportunity is presented to this wicked and profane man to #emph[bring forth the sin] that had lain in his heart for #emph[twenty years] now. He has full power in his hand to carry out his purpose. In the middle of this state of things, God comes in on his heart with some secret and effectual working of his Spirit and power. He turns him from his purpose and makes his conceived sin melt away, so that he falls on the neck of the man he had thought to kill and embraces him.

God’s dealing with #emph[Laban] the #emph[Aramean,] concerning the same #emph[Jacob,] was of the same nature, though the way of it was unusual (Gen 31:24). By a dream, a vision in the night, God keeps him from so much as speaking harshly to Jacob. It was with Laban as in Mic 2:1: he had devised evil on his bed, and when he intended to carry it out in the morning, God stepped in with a dream and hid sin from him, as it is put in Job 33:15, 16, 17. To the same purpose is what the #emph[psalmist] says about the people of God (Ps 106:46): “He made them objects of compassion to all who held them captive.” Men usually deal harshly with those they have taken captive in war. It was the way of old to rule captives with force and cruelty. Here God turns and changes their captors’ hearts, not in general to himself, but in this particular of regard for his people. And in this way, in general, God prevents the bringing forth of a world of sin every day. He #emph[sharpens arrows] of conviction against the spirits of men concerning the particular thing they are engaged in. Their #emph[hearts] are not changed as to sin, but their #emph[minds] are altered as to this or that sin. Perhaps they break the vessel they had fashioned and go to work on some other. Now, so that we may see a little into the ways in which God accomplishes this work, we must lay down the following considerations first.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, the general #emph[means] in which the material of #emph[restraining grace] consists, by which God prevents the bringing forth of sin in this way, lies in certain #emph[arguments] and reasonings presented to the mind of the sinner. By them he is led to abandon his purpose, and to change and alter his mind about the sin he had conceived. Reasons against it are presented to him, and they prevail on him to give up his design and abandon his purpose. This is the general way restraining grace works: by #emph[arguments] and reasonings rising up against committing conceived sin.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, no #emph[arguments] or reasonings as such, considered #emph[in their content,] are sufficient to stop or hinder any purpose of sinning, or to make conceived sin prove abortive, if the sinner has power and opportunity to bring it forth. They are not #emph[restraining grace] in themselves and on their own account. If they were, the administration and communication of grace, as grace, would be left to every man who is able to give advice against sin. Nothing is or can be called #emph[grace,] even common grace that may perish, except with respect to its particular relation to God. God, by the power of his Spirit making #emph[arguments] and reasons effectual and prevailing, turns into #emph[grace] (I mean grace of this kind) what was in itself and in its own nature mere reason. And that efficacy of the Spirit which the Lord puts forth in these persuasions and motives is what we call #emph[restraining grace.] With these things laid down, we will now consider some of the #emph[arguments] we find he has used for this end and purpose.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, God stops many men in their ways, once sin is conceived, by an argument taken from the #emph[difficulty,] if not the impossibility, of doing what they aim at. They have a mind to it, but God sets a hedge and a wall before them, so that they judge what they intend so hard and #emph[difficult] to accomplish that it is better for them to let it alone and give it up. So #emph[Herod] would have put #emph[John the Baptist] to death at the first provocation, but he “was afraid of the people, because they regarded John as a prophet” (Matt 14:5). He had conceived John’s murder and was free to carry it out. God raised this consideration in his heart: “If I kill him, the people will riot. He has a great following among them, and a revolt will arise that may cost me my life or my kingdom.” He “was afraid of the people,” and did not dare to carry out the wickedness he had conceived, because of the #emph[difficulty] he foresaw he would be entangled in. And God made the argument effectual for that season. Otherwise, we know that men will venture the utmost hazards to satisfy their lusts, as Herod also did afterward. The #emph[Pharisees] were in exactly the same state and condition (Matt 21:26). They would gladly have denounced the ministry of #emph[John,] but did not dare to for fear of the people. And in v. 46 of the same chapter they were deterred by the same argument from #emph[killing our Savior,] who had greatly provoked them by a parable that set out their deserved and approaching destruction. They did not dare to do it for #emph[fear of a riot] among the people, since the people looked on him as a prophet. In this way God overawes the hearts of countless persons in the world every day, and makes them give up attempting to bring forth the sins they had conceived. They would be sure to meet #emph[difficulties]\; indeed, it is likely that if they attempted it, they would find it impossible to accomplish. We owe much of our #emph[quiet] in this world to the efficacy the Holy Spirit gives this consideration in the hearts of men. Adulteries, robberies, and murders are forestalled and stifled by it. Men would engage in them daily, if they did not judge it impossible to fulfill what they aim at.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, God does it by an argument #emph[ab incommodo] [from the harm], from the #emph[harms,] evils, and troubles that will come upon men in the pursuit of sin. If they follow it, this or that #emph[harm] will follow: this trouble, this evil, temporal or eternal. This argument, as the Spirit of God handles it, is the great engine in his hand by which he throws up banks and sets bounds to the lusts of men, so that they do not break out to the confusion of all the order and beauty that still remains in the works of his hands. #emph[Paul] gives us the general meaning of this argument in Rom 2:14, 15: “Indeed, when Gentiles, who do not have the law, do by nature what the law requires, they are a law to themselves, even though they do not have the law. So they show that the work of the law is written on their hearts, their consciences also bearing witness, and their thoughts either accusing or defending them.” If any men in the world might be thought to be given up to pursue and fulfill all the sins their lusts can conceive, it is those who “do not have the law,” those to whom the written law of God does not announce the evil that goes with sin. But though they do not have it, says the apostle, they “show the work of it.” They do many things it requires, and refrain or abstain from many things it forbids, and so show its work and efficacy. But how is it that they do so? Why, their “thoughts accuse or defend them.” It comes from the considerations and arguments they have within themselves about sin and its #emph[consequences.] These prevail on them to abstain from many things their hearts would carry them into. For #emph[conscience] is a man’s judging of himself beforehand, with respect to the future judgment of God. So #emph[Felix] was staggered in his pursuit of sin when he trembled at #emph[Paul’s] preaching of righteousness and the judgment to come (Acts 24:25). So #emph[Job] tells us that the consideration of #emph[punishment from God] has a strong influence on the minds of men to keep them from sin (ch. 31:1, 2, 3). I have declared before how the Lord uses that consideration even toward his own, when they have broken the cords of his love and thrown off the rule of his grace for a season.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, God does this same work by making effectual an #emph[argument ab inutili] [from the useless], from the #emph[unprofitableness] of the thing men are engaged in. By this the #emph[brothers] of #emph[Joseph] were held back from killing him (Gen 37:26, 27). “What profit will we gain,” they say, “if we kill our brother and cover up his blood?” They would get nothing by it; it would bring them no advantage or satisfaction. The heads of this way in which God obstructs conceived sin, or the springs of these kinds of arguments, are so many and various that it is impossible to dwell on them one by one. There is nothing present or to come, nothing belonging to this life or another, nothing desirable or undesirable, nothing good or evil, from which an argument may not be taken at one time or another to obstruct sin.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Fourthly, God accomplishes this work by #emph[arguments] taken #emph[ab honesto] [from the honorable], from what is #emph[good] and #emph[honorable,] what is fitting, praiseworthy, and acceptable to himself. This is the great road on which he walks with the saints under their temptations, or when they conceive sin. He effectually brings back to their minds the consideration of all those springs and motives of obedience that are revealed and set before us in the gospel, some at one time, some at another. He reminds them of his own love, mercy, and kindness, his #emph[eternal love,] with the fruits of it that they themselves have shared in. He reminds them of the #emph[blood of his Son,] his cross, his sufferings, his tremendous undertaking in the work of mediation, and how his heart, love, honor, and name are concerned in their obedience. He reminds them of the #emph[love of the Spirit,] with all his comforts that they have shared in, and the privileges entrusted to them by him. He reminds them of the #emph[gospel,] its glory and beauty as revealed to their souls. He reminds them of the excellence and beauty of #emph[obedience,] of their doing the duty they owe to God, and of the peace, quietness, and calm of mind they have enjoyed in it. On the other side, he reminds them that sin is a #emph[provocation] to the eyes of his glory, saying in their hearts, “Do not do this detestable thing that I hate.” He reminds them that they #emph[wound] the Lord Christ and put him to shame; that they #emph[grieve] the Holy Spirit, by whom they are sealed for the day of redemption; that they #emph[defile] his dwelling place. He reminds them of the reproach, dishonor, and scandal they bring on the gospel and the profession of it. He reminds them of the #emph[terrors,] darkness, wounds, and lack of peace they may bring on their own souls. From these and similar considerations God puts a stop to the progress of the law of sin in the heart, so that it does not go on to #emph[bring forth] the evil it has conceived. I could give instances recorded in Scripture of #emph[arguments] of all these several kinds, but that would be too long a work for us, who are now engaged in a design of another nature. One or two examples may be mentioned, though. #emph[Joseph] resists his first temptation on one of these grounds (Gen 39:9): “So how could I do such a great evil and sin against God?” The evil of #emph[sinning against God,] his God: that consideration alone keeps him from the least inclination to his temptation. “It is #emph[sin against God,] to whom I owe all obedience, the God of my life and of all my mercies; I will not do it.” The argument by which #emph[Abigail] prevailed on #emph[David] to hold him back from #emph[avenging himself] and from murder was of the same nature (1 Sam 25:31), and he acknowledges that it was from the Lord (v. 32). I will add no more, for all the motives to duty we have in Scripture, made effectual by grace, are instances of this way of God’s proceeding.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Sometimes, I confess, God works secretly on the hearts of men with his own finger to stop the progress of sin, without the use and means of such arguments as those we have dwelt on. So he tells #emph[Abimelech] (Gen 20:6), “I have kept you from sinning against Me.” Now this could not have been done by any of the arguments we have dwelt on, because #emph[Abimelech] did not know that what he intended was sin. That is why he pleads that he did it “in the integrity of his heart and the innocence of his hands” (v. 5). God turned his will and thoughts around, so that he did not accomplish his intention; but by what ways or means is not revealed. Nor is it evident what course God took in changing #emph[Esau’s] heart when he came out against his brother to destroy him (Gen 33:4). Did he stir up in him a fresh spring of natural affection? Did he make him consider what grief he would bring by this means on his aged father, who loved him so tenderly? Or, having now grown great and wealthy, did Esau more and more despise the matter in dispute between him and his brother, and so utterly disregard it? It is not known. Perhaps God did it by an #emph[immediate,] powerful act of his Spirit on his heart, without any of these or similar considerations actually coming between. Now the things mentioned are, at other times, #emph[feeble] and weak in themselves. Yet when they are handled by the Spirit of God for such an end and purpose, they certainly become #emph[effectual,] and are the material of his #emph[preventing grace.]]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, God prevents the bringing forth of conceived sin by #emph[real, spiritual, saving grace,] either in the first conversion of sinners or in the further supplies of it that follow. This is one part of the mystery of his grace and love. He sometimes meets men in their highest resolutions for sin with the highest efficacy of his grace. By this he shows the #emph[power] of his own grace. And he gives the soul a further experience of the #emph[law of sin] when the soul takes its leave of that law by being changed in the middle of its resolutions to serve its lusts. By this he melts down the lusts of men and makes them wither at the root, so that they no longer strive to bring forth what they have #emph[conceived,] but are filled with shame and sorrow that they conceived it. We have an example and instance of this proceeding of God in #emph[Paul,] for the use and instruction of all generations. His heart was full of wickedness, blasphemy, and persecution. What he had conceived had come to rage and madness, and to a full purpose of exercising it all to the utmost; so the history relates it (#emph[Acts] 9). And so he himself declares his state to have been (Acts 26:9, 10, 11, 12; 1 Tim 1:13). In the middle of all this violent pursuit of sin, a #emph[voice from heaven] shuts up the womb and dries up its breasts, and he cries, “Lord, what do You want me to do?” (Acts 9:6). The same person seems to imply that this is God’s way of proceeding with others too: that he meets them with his #emph[converting grace] at the height of their sin and folly (1 Tim 1:16). For #emph[he himself,] he says, was an example of God’s dealing with others; as God dealt with him, so he would also do with some other such sinners. “But for this very reason I was shown mercy, so that in me, the worst of sinners, Christ Jesus might display His perfect patience as an example to those who would believe in Him for eternal life.” And we have not a few examples of it in our own days. Several persons who deliberately went to this or that place to mock and scoff at the preaching of the word have been met in the very place where they planned to serve their lusts and Satan, and have been cast down at the feet of God. This way of God’s dealing with sinners is set out at length in Job 33:15, 16, 17, 18. #emph[Dionysius] the #emph[Areopagite] is another instance of this work of God’s grace and love. #emph[Paul] is dragged either #emph[by] him or #emph[before] him to plead for his life as a proclaimer of #emph[foreign gods,] which at #emph[Athens] was death by the law. In the middle of this frame of spirit God meets Dionysius with #emph[converting grace.] Sin withers in the womb, and he joins himself to #emph[Paul] and his doctrine (Acts 17:18–34). We have a similar dealing toward #emph[Israel] in Hos 11:7, 8, 9, 10. But there is no need to dwell on more instances of this observation. God is pleased to leave no generation unconvinced of this truth, if only they attend to their own experiences and to the examples of this work of his mercy among them. Every day one or another is taken in the full purpose of his heart to go on in sin, in this or that sin, and is stopped in his course by the power of #emph[converting grace.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, God does it by the same grace in its #emph[renewed communications,] that is, by special #emph[assisting grace.] This is his common way of dealing with #emph[believers] in this case. It was declared before that they too, through the deceitfulness of sin, may be carried on to conceive this or that sin. God puts a stop to their progress, or rather to the prevailing power of the law of sin in them, by giving them the special help needed for their preservation and deliverance. As #emph[David] says of himself (Ps 73:2), “my feet had almost stumbled; my steps had nearly slipped.” He was at the very brink of unbelieving, despairing thoughts and conclusions about God’s providence in governing the world, and he was recovered from them, as he declares afterward. So it is with many a believer. He is often at the very brink, at the very door, of some folly or iniquity, when God steps in with the efficacy of #emph[actually assisting grace] and recovers such believers to an obedient frame of heart again. And this is a special work of Christ, in which he shows and exercises his faithfulness toward his own. Heb 2:18: “He is able to help those who are being tempted.” It is not a bare power that is meant, but a power clothed with mercy, a power put forth from a sense of what poor believers suffer under their temptations. And how does he exercise this #emph[merciful ability] toward us? Ch. 4:16: he gives out, and we find in him, “grace to help us in our time of need”\; timely help and assistance for our deliverance when we are ready to be overpowered by sin and temptation. When “lust has conceived” and is ready to “give birth,” when the soul lies at the brink of some iniquity, he gives #emph[timely help,] relief, deliverance, and safety. A great part of Christ’s care and faithfulness toward his poor saints lies here. He will not allow them to be harried by the power of sin, nor to be carried out into ways that will dishonor the gospel, or fill them with shame and reproach, and so make them useless in the world. Instead he steps in with the saving relief and #emph[assistance] of his grace, stops the course of sin, and makes them “more than conquerors in him.” And this assistance lies under the promise of 1 Cor 10:13: “No temptation has seized you#super[+] except what is common to man. And God is faithful; He will not let you#super[+] be tempted beyond what you#super[+] can bear. But when you#super[+] are tempted, He will also provide a way of escape, so that you#super[+] can stand up under it.” Temptation will try us; it is for our good, and the Lord brings about many holy ends by it. But when we are tried to the utmost of our ability, so that one more assault would overpower us, a “way of escape” is provided. This may be done in several ways, as I have declared elsewhere, and the way we are now speaking of is one of the most eminent: #emph[supplies of grace] to enable the soul to hold up, resist, and conquer. And once God begins to deal with a soul in this way of love, he will not stop adding one supply after another until the whole work of his grace and faithfulness is accomplished. We have an example of this in Isa 57:17, 18. Poor sinners there are so far held captive to the power of their lusts that God’s first and second dealings with them do not effectually deliver them. But he will not give up. He is pursuing a design of love toward them, and so he does not stop until they are recovered. These are the general heads of the second way in which God hinders the bringing forth of conceived sin, that is, by working on the #emph[will] of the sinner. He does it either by #emph[common convictions] or by #emph[special grace,] so that sinners let go of their own accord the purpose and will to sin that they had risen up to. And this is no small part of his providing for his own glory and for the honor of his gospel in the world. Its professors would stain all its beauty if they were left to themselves to bring forth all the evil #emph[conceived] in their hearts.]]

=== The Way of Afflictions

Besides these #emph[general] ways, there is one still more #emph[special,] which works on both the #emph[power] and the #emph[will] of the sinner at once. This is the way of #emph[afflictions,] and one word about it will close this discourse. #emph[Afflictions,] I say, work in both these ways with regard to #emph[conceived sin.] They work #emph[providentially] on the #emph[power] of the creature. When a man has conceived a sin and is fully set on pursuing it, God often sends a #emph[sickness] and weakens his strength, or a #emph[loss] that cuts him short in his plenty. So he takes the man off from the pursuit of his lusts, though perhaps his heart is not weaned from them. His #emph[power] is weakened, and he cannot do the evil he would. In this sense affliction belongs to the first way in which God forestalls the production of sin. Great #emph[afflictions] sometimes work, not immediately and directly from their own nature, but from the gracious purpose and intention of him who sends them. Into his dealing out of them he puts something of grace and power, of love and kindness, which effectually draws the heart and mind away from sin. Ps 119:67: “Before I was afflicted, I went astray; but now I keep Your word.” And in this way, because renewing and assisting grace predominates in them, afflictions belong to the second means of preventing sin.

These are some of the ways in which it pleases God to put a stop to the #emph[progress of sin,] both in believers and in unbelievers, that we will give as instances at present. And if we tried to search out his ways further, to perfection, we would still have to conclude that it is only a “little portion that we know of him.”
